#---
# purpose: "a demanded list slot keeps its element at every depth, so a nested-list accumulator does not blend two users"
#---
#
# The `ListShape` half of fz-kdt.183's element rule. `List.reduce_cont/3`
# escaped the old collapse only because its mask is `ListShape(Ignore)` and the
# descent reached the element; nest the element one list deeper and it
# collapsed too, because `convergence_collapse_list_shape` handed an `Ignore`
# element straight to the freight collapse.
#
# At HEAD that showed up not as a spec violation but as a materialization
# abort: the settled closure return `{:cont, [[binary] | [int]]}` disagreed
# with the semantic result `{:cont, [[binary]]} | {:cont, [[int]]}`, and the
# artifact stage panicked rather than emit a program built on two answers.
#
# Demand reaching a list's SHAPE means the element is meaning at every depth --
# nothing at the collapse can say how far down the chain that reads it looks --
# so the element is kept and the two users key apart.
#
# The two `type/no-matching-clause` warnings in the golden are fz-kdt.185's,
# as in `with_index_users_key_apart_by_element.fz`.
defmodule M do
  @spec strings([binary]) :: integer
  fn strings(_xs), do: 1
end

fn main() do
  a = Enum.with_index([[1], [2]], fn (x, _i) -> x end)
  b = Enum.with_index([["s"], ["t"]], fn (x, _i) -> x end)
  dbg(a)
  dbg(b)
end
